WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.215
Coordinates make geometric statements numerical. A position is meaningful relative to an origin and axes. A

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:14.230
displacement compares two positions measured in the same convention and records the change coordinate by coordinate.

00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:22.051
Both positions use the same origin, axis directions and coordinate units. Otherwise subtraction would not describe

00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:23.423
the intended move.

00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:30.717
The first coordinate is horizontal change; the second is vertical change. A negative change means movement

00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:32.413
opposite the positive axis.

00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:39.218
Can the displacement change while its vertical component stays constant? Pause here and make a prediction

00:00:39.218 --> 00:00:40.003
before continuing.

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:49.857
The displacement is a vector. Its Euclidean norm is a single nonnegative distance. For our starting

00:00:49.857 --> 00:00:54.763
inputs, Horizontal change: 3. Vertical change: -2. Distance: 3.6056.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:01:02.131
Adding a displacement to a position gives another position. Shifting the origin changes both positions but

00:01:02.131 --> 00:01:03.847
leaves their difference unchanged.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:11.261
Check what each coordinate represents before adding vectors. Feature vectors and spatial positions share notation but

00:01:11.261 --> 00:01:13.847
need not share a geometric meaning.

